        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a defendant maintains his innocence and insists on testing the prosecution on its burden of proof, the Constitution prohibits a trial court from permitting the defendant's own lawyer, over the defendant's objection, to tell the jury that he is guilty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But my particular question is obviously, when you say simply that my client shot the guy, that doesn't mean he's guilty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: That -- that's easy for you to say in a case that you're imagining. What I'm wondering, if there are other cases where it might be far more difficult to come up with that answer, and, therefore, I'm asking you this question: Suppose the opinion were to say in this case the lawyer explicitly said to the jury he is guilty of the crime charged. That the Sixth Amendment forbids."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Bar of England and Wales",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And we -- we go over this at some length in our brief. There's no controversion about this. I think that at the time in the 18th century in England and common law jurisdictions and at the time of the framing of the Sixth Amendment, the frame -- people would have been astonished, as the -- as the amicus brief of the bar of England and Wales expresses, that the notion that the defendant could say this is my defense and my decision to contest this invokes my own subjective judgments about what is important to me and what is not important to me, that it would be -- they would be astonished to hear that in that circumstance defense counsel could stand up and say -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Louisiana",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ELIZABETH MURRILL, SOLICITOR GENERAL OF LOUISIANA, ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT MS. MURRILL: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The state proposes a -- a rule that, in a narrow class of death penalty classes, counsel sometimes might be required to override his client on a trial strategy when the strategy that the -- that the client wants counsel to pursue is a futile charade and requires him to defeat both their objectives of defeating the death penalty. We submit that that should be treated as a Strickland ineffective assistance of counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: You'd agree, though, that he -- he effectively conceded guilt to second-degree murder?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I'm sorry, I thought it's been not disputed that he thought, quite wrongly, that there was no mens rea for second-degree murder but that it's been conceded that what -- there -- that there was a mens rea both for first and second degree and he was only arguing for second degree?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Murrill",
      "case_id": "2017-16-8255",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cfb0236509cb-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MURRILL: That in a very narrow class of death penalty cases, counsel may be required to override the decision of his client, if that's -- if -- if the client's strategy is -- is futile and -\u00ad"
    },
